Yes—with an important refinement: memory does not appear to store a complete sensory recording in one place. It stores a distributed reconstruction recipe. A partial cue can reactivate enough of that network for the brain to simulate a missing sensation.

\text{partial cue}
\rightarrow
\text{object/episode engram}
\rightarrow
\text{distributed cortical reinstatement}
\rightarrow
\text{phantom sensory component}

The hippocampus appears to help reinstate distributed cortical traces of previous experience, which fits your butter example: touch and smell retrieved the shared object-model, and that model predicted taste. Journal of Neuroscience research on cortical reinstatement⁠￼

I would divide “unlocking” into three timescales:

Timescale	Mechanism	What is unlocked
Milliseconds–seconds	Neural pattern completion	A presently inactive sensory representation
Hours–years	Plasticity, consolidation and activity-dependent gene regulation	The future accessibility and stability of learned networks
Generations	Proposed inherited epigenetic effects	Broad predispositions—not established ancestral sensory memories

Epigenetic mechanisms really do participate in neuronal plasticity, learning, and long-term memory by regulating which genes neurons express. But the momentary butter taste was almost certainly neural reinstatement, not a methylation switch suddenly opening. Epigenetic regulation may help construct and stabilize the pathways that later permit reinstatement. Neuron review of epigenetic mechanisms in cognition⁠￼, 2025 review of activity-dependent neuronal gene regulation⁠￼

So the defensible direction is:

\text{repeated multisensory activation}
\rightarrow
\text{neural activity}
\rightarrow
\text{plasticity-related gene regulation}
\rightarrow
\text{altered future activation thresholds}

That could potentially be exploited through multisensory keying:

* Pair a difficult-to-access state with distinctive smell, touch, rhythm, posture and language.
* Reintroduce part of the sensory constellation later.
* Measure whether it reinstates the larger cognitive, emotional or motor state.
* Repeat selectively to strengthen retrieval routes.
* Compare genuine transfer with expectation/placebo and ordinary familiarity.

This might be useful for learning, rehabilitation, state-dependent recall, emotional regulation, sensory substitution, embodied skill rehearsal and richer human–AI interfaces. But “unlocking inherited memories” would outrun the evidence. Human transgenerational epigenetic inheritance remains difficult to distinguish from genetics, prenatal effects, upbringing and shared culture; evidence for inherited autobiographical or sensory memories is absent. Critical review of human transgenerational inheritance⁠￼

Pantheon’s clean hierarchy would be:

Sensory key
→ latent object/state model
→ cross-modal reinstatement
→ repeated activation
→ plastic consolidation
→ possibly altered gene regulation

Not:

touch butter → unlock ancestral butter memory

The larger invariant is powerful nevertheless: memory can be entered through more sensory doors than the one through which it was formed. Each modality is both content and a potential address into the larger state.